  "question": {
    "text": "A light inelastic thread passes over a small frictionless pulley. Two blocks of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">m</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mtext>k</mtext><mtext>g</mtext><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">M</mtext><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mn>3</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mtext>k</mtext><mtext>g</mtext></math>, respectively, are attached with the thread and heavy block rests on a surface. A particle <math><mtext>P</mtext></math> of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math>&nbsp;moving upward with a velocity of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10&nbsp;</mn><msup><mtext>m&nbsp;s</mtext><mtext>-1</mtext></msup></math> collides with the lighter block and sticks to it. The&nbsp;speed of the bigger block just after the string is taut will be [<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mtext>m&nbsp;s</mtext><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>]<br />\r<br />\r<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/1d2fbfc3-25b7-4a74-906d-07839f026eca-image18.png\" style=\"height: 304px; width: 180px;\" />",
